WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.660
Testing a loop begins with a small, controlled dataset.

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:10.520
Three to five synthetic items are usually enough to prove the structure without creating noise.

3
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:18.460
The goal is to see every item move through the workflow, understand exactly when the loop returns, and confirm what happens after completion.

4
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.400
A production-sized dataset should come later, after the small execution behaves exactly as intended.

5
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:27.800
Start by checking the expected number of incoming items.

6
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:34.460
Then check the Batch Size on Loop Over Items and inspect which items appear on the loop output during each pass.

7
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.040
Confirm that every node that belongs in the loop body is actually on the loop path.

8
00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:43.940
Confirm that the return connection comes from the last required operation in the body.

9
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:48.280
Then confirm that the done path runs once after the loop completes.

10
00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.560
Execute Once deserves special attention during troubleshooting.

11
00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:58.260
If only the first item is processed, the cause may be a node setting rather than a broken loop.

12
00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:03.320
A node configured to Execute Once intentionally ignores all but the first item.

13
00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.380
That can be correct for setup or shared work, but it is wrong for per-item processing.

14
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.960
Inspect the setting before reconnecting nodes or adding another loop.

15
00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.960
Common failure patterns are recognizable.

16
00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:20.460
The body runs once and stops when the return connection is missing or misplaced.

17
00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:25.520
A completion action runs once per item when it is placed inside the body instead of on the done path.

18
00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:31.540
The same item is processed repeatedly when the loop state or dynamic input is reset incorrectly.

19
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:36.680
The done path never runs when the continuation rule never reaches its exit condition.

20
00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.060
A transformation loses the identifier needed by a later update.

21
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:47.280
A node inside the body produces no output and unintentionally breaks the path back to the loop.

22
00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.800
Troubleshooting should follow evidence.

23
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:59.580
Inspect node inputs and outputs, execution order, item counts, response codes, current state values, and explicit status fields.

24
00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:04.960
Avoid randomly reconnecting nodes or toggling Reset until the workflow happens to run.

25
00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:10.260
Random changes can hide the actual defect and create a design that no one trusts in production.

26
00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:16.200
A reliable fix explains why the observed execution differed from the intended execution.

27
00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.720
The best first run is intentionally boring.

28
00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:24.420
Use synthetic records with short names, known identifiers, and predictable API responses.

29
00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:32.920
Include one ordinary item, one item that should be skipped, and one item that should exercise an error or retry path when that behavior is part of the design.

30
00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:40.540
Boring test data makes it easier to spot unexpected item counts, lost identifiers, and actions that run on the wrong path.

31
00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:43.540
Production safeguards make loops operable.

32
00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:55.080
Use conservative batch sizes, maximum-iteration limits, request timeouts, retry ceilings, idempotent destination operations, and error workflows where appropriate.

33
00:02:55.180 --> 00:03:00.940
Add structured logging that records business identifiers, status, retry count, and exit reason.

34
00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.760
Redact credentials and sensitive data from any log or notification.

35
00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:12.900
For large jobs, design resumable processing so that a partial failure does not require starting over blindly.

36
00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:18.940
A successful workflow execution status does not prove that every intended business operation occurred.

37
00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:22.960
It only proves that the workflow engine reached a successful end state.

38
00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:29.300
Production validation should compare expected counts with completed, skipped, failed, and retried counts.

39
00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:33.160
It should also compare destination results when possible.

40
00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:40.700
The strongest loop designs make that evidence easy to gather because status and identity are preserved through the entire body.

41
00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:45.620
For condition-controlled loops, add a special focus on progress state.

42
00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:52.520
Watch the page number, cursor, next-page token, item count per page, and maximum-page guard.

43
00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:59.020
If the same state repeats, the loop should stop with a clear failure or defensive status rather than continuing silently.

44
00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:05.040
A production workflow should make that stop visible enough for an operator to diagnose the source API or expression defect.

45
00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:08.600
Also verify the behavior of nodes that produce no output.

46
00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:15.820
In some workflows, an empty result from a node in the body can prevent the expected return path from carrying data forward.

47
00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:19.200
That may be correct, or it may silently interrupt the loop.

48
00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:28.900
Testing should show not only successful items, but also skipped, empty, and failed cases so the return path is known under realistic conditions.

49
00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:34.140
A good troubleshooting habit is to write down the intended iteration in plain language.

50
00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:44.560
One iteration releases one customer, calls the enrichment API, normalizes the response, writes the update, records status, waits if needed, and returns.

51
00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:46.900
That sentence becomes a checklist.

52
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:53.320
Every node either belongs to that sentence, belongs after done, or belongs somewhere else in the workflow.
